Webhook Delivery Flow

  1. Event Publishing

  User/Producer → POST /api/v1/events/publish → Event created in DB
  - Event is published with a schema ID and payload
  - System finds all active subscriptions for that schema
  - Creates delivery queue entries for each subscription

  2. Delivery Queue

  subscriptions table → delivery_queue table
  - Each subscription that matches the event schema gets a delivery entry
  - Status starts as queued
  - Includes: event data, subscriber webhook URL, retry config

  3. Webhook Worker Processing

  webhook_delivery_worker.py (polls every 2 seconds)
  The worker running in the background:
  - Polls delivery_queue for entries ready to send (status = queued or retries due)
  - Batches up to 10 deliveries per poll
  - Sends HTTP POST to subscriber's webhook URL
  - Includes HMAC signature in X-Webhook-Signature header

  4. Delivery Attempt

  POST https://subscriber-webhook-url.com/webhook
  Headers:
    X-Webhook-Signature: sha256=<hmac_hash>
    Content-Type: application/json
  Body:
    {event payload}

  5. Retry Logic (if delivery fails)

  Backoff Strategies:
  - Exponential (default): 1s → 2s → 4s → 8s → 16s → 32s...
  - Linear: 5s → 10s → 15s → 20s → 25s...
  - Fixed: 30s delay for all retries

  Max Retries: Configured per subscription (default: 3)

  Status Flow:
  queued → processing → failed → retrying → ...
                       ↓
                     completed (success)
                       ↓
                     failed (max retries) → DLQ

  6. Delivery Logs

  Every attempt is logged in delivery_logs table:
  - Request/response details
  - Latency
  - Status codes
  - Error messages
  - Retry attempt number

  7. Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)

  When max retries exceeded:
  - Delivery moved to delivery_dlq table
  - Can be manually retried later
  - Useful for debugging persistent failures

  8. Statistics Tracking

  Updates on subscriptions and event_messages tables:
  - total_deliveries
  - successful_deliveries
  - failed_deliveries
  - Success rate calculations

  Current System Status

  You can check the worker status:
  tail -f /tmp/webhook_worker.log

  Or check the queue:
  docker exec webhook-postgres psql -U webhook_user -d webhook_db -c "SELECT status, COUNT(*) FROM
  delivery_queue GROUP BY status;"